wasm jit: compile callers of System.Reflection; keep stack walkers right (gex)

The "residual reflection" refusal kept every method with a residual call into
System.Reflection in the interpreter. It
guards a re-entry into a dynamic type mid-Finish, so it now applies only once a
Reflection.Emit image exists (mono_dynamic_images_exist).

Lifting it exposed what the namespace check had been hiding: JIT frames are invisible
to stack walks. Now, as llvm_only does:
- MethodBase.GetCurrentMethod and Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly are intrinsics under
  COMPILE_WASM (intrinsics.c);
- a method calling a stack-walking callee (REQSECOBJ, Type.GetType) is refused;
- a REQSECOBJ method itself is refused (GetExecutingAssembly compiled by the JIT
  answered System.Private.CoreLib to an interpreted caller).
Test: T.Reflect (ReflGet, StackWalkers).

diff --git a/src/mono/mono/metadata/dynamic-image.c b/src/mono/mono/metadata/dynamic-image.c
index b4405d78c..8d2c01762 100644
--- a/src/mono/mono/metadata/dynamic-image.c
+++ b/src/mono/mono/metadata/dynamic-image.c
@@ -46,6 +46,16 @@ dynamic_images_unlock (void)
 	mono_os_mutex_unlock (&dynamic_images_mutex);
 }
 
+/* gex: whether any System.Reflection.Emit image was ever created. The wasm JIT keeps its
+ * "residual reflection" refusal only then (mini-wasm-emitter.inc). An unlocked read: the
+ * array is created once and only grows. */
+gboolean
+mono_dynamic_images_exist (void)
+{
+	GPtrArray *a = dynamic_images;
+	return a && a->len;
+}
+
 void
 mono_dynamic_images_init (void)
 {
